    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2689.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 684,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to truly understand water, we have to look at it through a few different lenses: its unique physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (Why Water is Weird and Wonderful)\nWater is one of the most unique substances in the universe. Unlike almost every other chemical, it behaves in ways that defy normal rules:\n\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is how it carries vital nutrients through our bodies and minerals through the earth.\n* **Density Anomaly:** When most liquids freeze, they contract and become denser. Water actually **expands** and becomes *less* dense when it freezes. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the \"Blue Planet\" because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **The Oceans:** About 96.5% of all Earth's water is held in the oceans as salt water.\n* **Freshwater:** Only about 2.5% is freshwater. Most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground, leaving less than 1% easily accessible for human use in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed system powered by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the ground as rain or snow (precipitation), and flows back into bodies of water. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* **Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It cushions our joints, regulates our internal temperature, flushes out waste, and enables our cells to transport nutrients and chemical signals.\n* **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of species of plants and animals, ranging from microscopic plankton to the blue whale.\n* **Human Civilization:** Throughout history, human civilizations have always arisen near water sources—rivers, lakes, and coasts—for drinking, agriculture, hygiene, and transportation.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It is the lifeblood of our planet, the driving force behind weather and geology, and the essential ingredient that makes biological life possible."
